1974	## Regarding the dashed vertical line + dot at the hover point (your key question)
1975	
1976	**I searched exhaustively and confirmed this does NOT exist in this codebase**, on this branch, in the current-gen Graphs module family (`RamUsageGraph`, `CpuUtilizationGraph`, `BandwidthUsageGraph`, `NetworkSpeedGraph`, `DiskSpaceGraph`, and the shared `Graphs/*` components):
1977	
1978	- No `<ReferenceLine>` import/usage anywhere in `src/components/Graphs` or any of the 4 chart components that use recharts.
1979	- No `activeDot` prop customization on any `<Area>`/`<Bar>`/`<Line>` — all use `dot={false}` and rely on recharts' default (invisible/default) active-dot behavior, i.e. no custom dot marker rendering is wired up.
1980	- No `cursor` prop customization on any `<Tooltip>` — all use bare `<Tooltip content={<GraphTimeTooltip .../>} />`, meaning recharts renders its **default tooltip cursor** (for `AreaChart`/`ComposedChart` this defaults to a thin solid vertical line, not dashed, and with no dot) as the only "on-chart" hover indicator.
1981	- The only `strokeDasharray` usages anywhere in these files are on `<CartesianGrid vertical={false} strokeDasharray="5 5" />` — the static dashed *background grid* (horizontal lines only, since `vertical={false}`), which has nothing to do with hover interaction.
1982	- `GraphTimeTooltip` (and its `TooltipStack` wrapper) is a floating box only — positioned via recharts' default tooltip positioning algorithm, not pinned to the bottom of a reference line. It does not render an SVG line, dot, or any chart-overlay graphic itself — it's pure `Typography` inside a styled `Stack`.
1983	
1984	**Conclusion:** the "dashed vertical line down to the x-axis with a dot marker and a floating date label near the bottom" pattern you saw in the screenshot is **not implemented in this repo as it stands on `feature/epic-server-monitoring-dashboard`**. Either (a) it's from a different/newer iteration not yet merged to this branch, (b) it's a design mock/Figma reference rather than shipped code, or (c) recharts' *default* tooltip cursor behavior (typically a solid line, not dashed, no dot) was being interpreted as this pattern in the screenshot. If you want to recreate that exact interaction in your portfolio site, you'd need to build it yourself — the standard recharts approach is to pass a custom `cursor` prop to `<Tooltip>` (e.g. a custom-rendered vertical `<line strokeDasharray="...">` plus a `<circle>` dot at the active point's y-value) and/or use `<ReferenceLine x={activeX} .../>` driven by the same `activeIndex`/`onMouseMove` state these components already track, then position a label near `y2` of that line instead of relying on the default floating-tooltip placement. The `activeIndex` state pattern (`onMouseMove` → `state.activeTooltipIndex`, `onMouseLeave` → reset) used in all four recharts-based components here is exactly the hook point you'd use to drive such a custom overlay, since it's already tracking the hovered data index and is passed through to compute the `GraphTimeTooltip`'s date label — the date format logic in `graphMeasurements.ts`'s `tooltipDateFormat` (`'MMM D, YYYY'` for `'2week'` duration) is what would produce a "May 9, 2026"-style label if that duration were selected.
