Do NOT reuse `#0B7FA8`/`#2DC7FF` (Undercurrent's brand) — Watchtower's brand pair from frontmatter is `#E17100` light / `#FD9A00` dark. Since this dashboard has no interactive brand-color affordance (unlike Undercurrent's variant switcher), the brand pair is only relevant if any chrome element (e.g. the (i) info icon color) should tie back to the case-study's accent — recommend using it for the info icons specifically (`--wt-demo-icon-accent: #E17100` / `#FD9A00`), everything else uses the neutral/status/series palette above. Components consume tokens via inline `style={{ color: "var(--wt-demo-...)" }}`, same convention as `UcCard.tsx`.

## 4. The custom hover overlay — recommended approach

**Recommendation: custom `content` renderer on `<Tooltip>` combined with a `<Customized>` (or manually-rendered `<g>` inside the chart via a render-prop layer) that draws the dashed line + dot, NOT the `cursor` prop and NOT `ReferenceLine`.**

### Why not the alternatives

- **`cursor` prop on `<Tooltip>`**: `cursor` is designed to render a shape following the mouse/active point (recharts passes it `points`, `width`, `height` for cartesian charts), and CAN render an arbitrary custom element (`cursor={<CustomCursor />}`). This gets the dashed line "for free" style-wise. But it does not give a clean way to also render the floating date label anchored below the line at the same time — `cursor` and `content` are two separate slots that both get invoked per-hover but don't share layout coordinates with each other directly, and `cursor`'s render function only receives cursor geometry, not the tooltip payload (label/value). You'd end up needing a second overlay for the label anyway, defeating the "one clean mechanism" goal.
- **`<ReferenceLine>`**: `ReferenceLine` is meant for static or slowly-changing reference marks (e.g. a threshold line), positioned via a fixed `x`/`y` value from data. Driving it from `activeIndex` on every mouse move works, but `ReferenceLine` doesn't give you a dot marker or label positioning primitives beyond its own basic `label` prop (which is a simple text-at-one-end, not a floating box anchored at a specific pixel position below the line). It's the wrong abstraction — reference lines are semantically "this data value is significant," not "here's what's under the cursor."
- **A fully separate absolutely-positioned `<div>` overlay outside the SVG**, driven purely by `onMouseMove`'s reported `activeCoordinate`: this actually works and is simpler to reason about for the *label* (a real DOM `<div>` with `position: absolute`, easy to style with Tailwind/CSS, no SVG text-measuring headaches) — but doing it for the *line + dot* too means duplicating recharts' internal coordinate math outside the chart's own coordinate system (margins, axis widths change per-card), which is fragile across the 5 differently-sized cards. Worth doing for the label only (see below), not the line/dot.

### Chosen approach in detail

Use recharts' **`onMouseMove` callback on the chart** (already the pattern for driving `activeIndex`, per the Wallaby reference) combined with **rendering the dashed line + dot as a custom SVG layer inside the chart via `<Customized component={...} />`** (recharts' officially supported "give me a render-prop with access to internal chart state/offset/xAxisMap" extension point), and rendering the **floating date label as a plain absolutely-positioned `<div>`** sibling to the chart, positioned using the pixel coordinate recharts reports via `onMouseMove`'s `state.activeCoordinate` (or equivalently `state.chartX`).

Concretely, in `ChartHoverOverlay.tsx`:

```tsx
// ChartHoverOverlay.tsx
interface ChartHoverOverlayProps {
  activeIndex: number | null;
  data: Array<{ time: number; [key: string]: number }>;
  xAccessorKey?: string; // defaults to "time"
}

// Rendered as a <Customized component={...} /> child of the chart:
// recharts calls this render function with { xAxisMap, yAxisMap, offset }
// giving pixel-space access to plot the vertical dashed line + dot at
// activeIndex's x position, spanning the plot's full drawable height
// (offset.top to offset.top + offset.height).
function HoverLineLayer({ activeIndex, data, xAxisMap, yAxisMap, offset, dataKey }) {
  if (activeIndex == null) return null;
  const point = data[activeIndex];
  const xAxis = Object.values(xAxisMap)[0];
  const yAxis = Object.values(yAxisMap)[0];
  const x = xAxis.scale(point.time);
  const y = yAxis.scale(point[dataKey]);
  const bottomY = offset.top + offset.height;

  return (
    <g pointerEvents="none">
      <line
        x1={x} x2={x}
        y1={offset.top} y2={bottomY}
        stroke="var(--wt-demo-hover-line)"
        strokeDasharray="4 4"
        strokeWidth={1}
      />
      <circle
        cx={x} cy={y} r={4}
        fill="var(--wt-demo-chart-ram)" /* series color, passed as prop */
        stroke="var(--wt-demo-hover-dot-stroke)"
        strokeWidth={2}
      />
    </g>
  );
}
```

The chart's `<Tooltip>` itself is rendered with `content={() => null}` (or omitted entirely) — recharts still needs *some* tooltip-adjacent mechanism to report `isTooltipActive`/`activeTooltipIndex` via `onMouseMove`, which it does regardless of whether `content` renders anything, so no tooltip box is needed at all; all the "tooltip" visual work is done by the custom line/dot layer plus the external label div.

For the label itself (only needed on RAM per the Figma reference, but built generically so any card can opt in), the parent card component (`RamUsageCard.tsx`) tracks the same `activeIndex` plus captures pixel coordinates from `onMouseMove`'s `state.chartX`/`state.chartY` (recharts reports these directly on the mouse-move state object, no extra computation needed), then renders:

```tsx
{activeIndex != null && hoverX != null && (
  <div
    className="pointer-events-none absolute -translate-x-1/2 rounded px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-mono"
    style={{
      left: hoverX,
      top: chartBottomY + 6, // just below the plot area / dashed line's bottom end
      backgroundColor: "var(--wt-demo-neutral-bg-weak)",
      color: "var(--wt-demo-neutral-text-weak)",
    }}
  >
    {formatTooltipDate(data[activeIndex].time)}
  </div>
)}
```

This requires the chart's wrapping `<div>` to be `position: relative` (standard for this pattern) so the label's `position: absolute` is scoped to the chart, not the whole card.

### Trade-offs summary

- `<Customized>` is the only recharts extension point that hands back real plot-area pixel geometry (`xAxisMap`/`yAxisMap`/`offset`) without recomputing scales manually — this is why it's preferred over a hand-rolled overlay for the line+dot, which live *inside* the SVG coordinate space and benefit from that.
- The date label lives *outside* the SVG as a plain `<div>` because floating text with padding/background/rounded corners is far more natural in HTML/CSS than SVG `<text>` + manual `<rect>` sizing, and recharts' `onMouseMove` state already hands back the exact pixel `chartX`/`chartY` needed to position it — no need to duplicate scale math for this piece.
- This keeps exactly one source of truth (`activeIndex`, set once in `onMouseMove`) driving three things: the stat-highlight numbers above the chart, the dashed-line+dot layer, and the floating label — matching the "same `activeIndex` drives everything" pattern from the Wallaby reference.
- `<Tooltip content={() => null}>` (or simply omitting `<Tooltip>` and driving everything off `onMouseMove` directly) is intentional — recharts's built-in tooltip box is not used at all in this design, only its hover-tracking side effects.

This same `HoverLineLayer` + label pattern should be extracted generically enough that `CpuUtilizationCard` and `NetworkSpeedCard` can reuse it too (their Figma refs don't show the label explicitly, but the dashed line + dot interaction should feel consistent across all chart cards) — `RamUsageCard` is simply the one that also renders the label, controlled by a prop like `showHoverLabel`.

## 5. Card grid layout

In `WatchtowerModulesSandbox.tsx`, wrap the 5 cards in a single CSS grid, not two separate flex rows, so the fluid column tracks stay consistent:

```tsx
<div className="wt-demo grid grid-cols-1 gap-[var(--wt-demo-space-200)] sm:grid-cols-2 lg:[grid-template-columns:repeat(6,1fr)]">
  <div className="lg:col-span-2"><CpuUtilizationCard /></div>
  <div className="lg:col-span-2"><RamUsageCard /></div>
  <div className="lg:col-span-2"><DiskSpaceCard /></div>
  <div className="sm:col-span-2 lg:col-span-3"><BandwidthCard /></div>
  <div className="sm:col-span-2 lg:col-span-3"><NetworkSpeedCard /></div>
</div>
```

Rationale: a 6-column `fr` track at `lg` lets row 1 be 3 equal `2fr` cards and row 2 be 2 equal `3fr` (wider) cards, all fluid — no fixed pixel widths, and it naturally fills whatever the container is (works whether that's 1000px at `lg` or narrower). Below `lg` (mobile/tablet), collapse to 2 columns (pairs stack reasonably) and below `sm`, single column (all 5 stacked). Each card wrapper should have `min-w-0` implicitly via grid item default sizing so chart `ResponsiveContainer`s never overflow. Every chart card's actual `<ResponsiveContainer width="100%" height={...}>` handles the fluid width inside each card; only the outer grid needs fixed fr-tracks. Recommend a fixed `height` per chart (e.g. `160px`–`180px`) rather than aspect-ratio, since Recharts' `ResponsiveContainer` needs an explicit height (percentage heights require a sized parent) and the Figma's cards are visually a fixed height regardless of width.

## 6. Data/math approach

Port directly to `graphMeasurements.ts` as plain, framework-free TS functions:

```ts
export function avg(data: Array<Record<string, number>>, key: string): number
export function computeChange(
  data: Array<Record<string, number>>,
  key: string,
  index: number,
): number | "new" | undefined
export function periodChange(currentAvg: number, prevAvg: number): number
export function changeColorForIncrease(changePercent: number): string // returns a var(--wt-demo-danger-text) / var(--wt-demo-success-text) CSS-var string, or accept a "polarity" flag per-card since e.g. CPU increase = concerning but bandwidth-incoming increase = neutral/expected — recommend adding a `direction: 'increase-is-bad' | 'increase-is-good'` param rather than hardcoding always-danger-on-increase, since Wallaby's servers-only context doesn't map 1:1 onto all 5 of these cards (e.g. Network speed "Incoming +20%" is shown with an upward/positive-feeling trend, not red)
export function formatTooltipDate(unixSeconds: number): string // Intl.DateTimeFormat("en-US", { month: "short", day: "numeric", year: "numeric" }).format(new Date(unixSeconds * 1000))
```

Each card component owns a small piece of local logic (not shared, since the "what does hovering show vs. what does idle show" swap is per-card and varies which fields are involved):

```tsx
const activeIndex = ...; // from onMouseMove, useState<number | null>
const displayValue = activeIndex != null
  ? data[activeIndex].cpu
  : avg(data, "cpu");
const displayChange = activeIndex != null
  ? computeChange(data, "cpu", activeIndex)
  : periodChange(avg(recentHalf, "cpu"), avg(earlierHalf, "cpu"));
```

This mirrors Wallaby's pattern (hover shows point-to-previous-point change; idle shows period-over-period average change) exactly, ported to hooks-only state with no MUI. `StatHighlight` itself stays a pure presentational component — all of this switching logic lives in each card, which then passes the resolved `value`/`changePercent`/`changeLabel` down as plain props.

## 7. Verification plan

1. `bun add recharts`, then `bun run astro check` and `bun run lint` to catch type/lint issues before visual check (repo has `eslint-plugin-react-hooks` active, relevant since hover state uses `useState`/event handlers).
2. `bun run dev`, navigate to the Watchtower case study page (`/projects/watchtower` or equivalent route — confirm exact route from `src/content/projects/` → routing config if unsure) and scroll to the "Strengthen the building blocks" section.
3. Visual pass at desktop width (~1120px window): confirm 3-card row 1 / 2-card row 2 layout matches Figma proportions, all 5 card headers/icons render, StatHighlight numbers match mock data.
4. Hover pass: hover across CPU, RAM, Bandwidth, Network speed charts (Disk space has no chart, skip) — confirm dashed line + dot track the cursor smoothly, RAM's floating date label appears and stays anchored under the dashed line's bottom end, and StatHighlight numbers above each chart swap from period-average to hovered-point values in sync with the cursor. Confirm `onMouseLeave` resets all cards back to idle/average state.
5. Toggle the site's dark/light mode (`.dark` class toggle — find the existing theme toggle control already used elsewhere on the site) with the dashboard in view; confirm every token in `watchtower-demo.css` resolves correctly in both states (no invisible text, chart series colors remain distinguishable, red danger states in Disk space stay legible).
6. Resize the browser down through `lg` → `sm` → below `sm` breakpoints (or use devtools responsive mode at ~768px, ~480px, ~375px); confirm the grid collapses to 2-col then 1-col without any chart overflowing its card or `ResponsiveContainer` throwing width-0 warnings in the console.
7. Check browser console for recharts warnings (common ones: missing `key` on `Cell`, `ResponsiveContainer` needing explicit dimensions, `Customized` component prop-shape mismatches) and resolve before considering the task done.

