Both files are at:
- `/Users/trounceabout/Documents/Repos/Personal/internal-transit/src/components/Lightbox.astro` (the overlay — note: it's directly in `src/components/`, not `src/components/case-study/`)
- `/Users/trounceabout/Documents/Repos/Personal/internal-transit/src/components/case-study/LightboxImage.astro` (the thumbnail trigger)

## Show/hide mechanism

It's a **class-toggle + `hidden` utility class** hybrid — not a native `<dialog>`, not a raw `display:none` swap alone. The backdrop is a plain `<div id="lightbox">` (`role="dialog" aria-modal="true"`), and visibility is driven by two Tailwind/CSS classes working together:

- Base state: `class="lightbox-backdrop fixed inset-0 z-50 hidden items-center justify-center p-6 sm:p-20"` — Tailwind's `hidden` (`display:none`) keeps it fully out of the layout/paint when closed, and CSS gives it `opacity: 0` too.
- **Opening** (`openLightbox()` in the `<script>` block):
  ```js
  backdrop.classList.remove("hidden");
  // Force layout before adding .is-open so the opacity/transform
  // transition actually runs instead of snapping straight to the open
  // state (removing `hidden` and adding the transition class in the same
  // tick would otherwise skip the transition on the first paint).
  requestAnimationFrame(() => backdrop.classList.add("is-open"));
  ```
- **Closing** (`closeLightbox()`):
  ```js
  backdrop.classList.remove("is-open");
  const finish = () => {
    backdrop.classList.add("hidden");
    media.replaceChildren();
    document.body.style.overflow = "";
    lastFocusedTrigger?.focus();
    lastFocusedTrigger = null;
  };
  if (prefersReducedMotion()) {
    finish();
  } else {
    backdrop.addEventListener("transitionend", finish, { once: true });
  }
  ```
  So it removes `is-open` immediately (triggering the fade/scale-out transition), and only re-adds `hidden` (and clears the media/scroll-lock/focus) once the CSS `transitionend` fires — or immediately if `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`.

## Triggers

- **Open**: delegated `document.addEventListener("click", ...)` looks for `.closest("[data-lightbox-trigger]")`. `LightboxImage.astro` renders a `<span data-lightbox-trigger data-lightbox-type="image" role="button" tabindex="0">` as that trigger. A parallel `keydown` listener manually handles Enter/Space activation for non-`<button>` triggers (the span), since it's not natively focusable/activatable like a real button (with a documented workaround for double-firing on the Loom overview `<button>` trigger elsewhere).
- **Close**: click on `#lightbox-close` (a `PillButton`), a click on the backdrop itself (`e.target === backdrop`), or `Escape` keydown (only while `is-open`). There's also a minimal focus trap on `focusin` that yanks focus back to the close button if it ever lands outside `#lightbox-content` while open.

## Existing transition/animation CSS

Yes, both the backdrop and the inner content box already animate, defined in `Lightbox.astro`'s `<style>` block:

```css
.lightbox-backdrop {
  background-color: var(--lightbox-backdrop);
  opacity: 0;
  transition: opacity 200ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1);
}

.lightbox-backdrop.is-open {
  display: flex;
  opacity: 1;
}

.lightbox-content {
  opacity: 0;
  transform: scale(0.96);
  transition:
    opacity 200ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1),
    transform 200ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1);
}

.lightbox-backdrop.is-open .lightbox-content {
  opacity: 1;
  transform: scale(1);
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .lightbox-backdrop,
  .lightbox-content {
    transition-duration: 0.01ms;
  }
}
```

So: backdrop fades opacity 0→1 (200ms, custom ease-out-ish cubic-bezier), and the inner `#lightbox-content` box both fades and scales up from `0.96` → `1` over the same duration/easing. `closeLightbox()`'s use of `transitionend` on the backdrop is what times the final `hidden` class re-add to match this CSS transition, and reduced-motion is respected both in CSS (`transition-duration: 0.01ms`) and in JS (`prefersReducedMotion()` skips waiting for `transitionend` and calls `finish()` synchronously).

`LightboxImage.astro` itself has its own separate, unrelated hover/focus transitions (a darkening `::after` scrim and an `ArrowsScale` expand icon, each `opacity 150ms ease`, also reduced-motion-aware) — these are the thumbnail's "click to enlarge" affordance, not part of the lightbox open/close mechanism itself.

