content.text:
Found the actual bug. Look at the CSS again:

```css
.lightbox-backdrop {
  opacity: 0;
  transition: opacity 300ms ...;
}
.lightbox-backdrop.is-open {
  display: flex;
  opacity: 1;
}
```

When `closeLightbox()` removes `is-open`, the backdrop's `display` property reverts from `flex` back to whatever `.lightbox-backdrop`'s own base rule implies — but the base `.lightbox-backdrop` rule has **no `display` declared at all**, so it falls back to the *element's* other class, `hidden` (Tailwind's `display: none`)... except `hidden` hasn't been re-added yet at that point, it's only added later inside `finish()`. So actually `display` should fall through to the default block value, not none — that's not it either.

The real issue: **`transitionend` only fires if a transition actually *runs***. And a transition only runs if the property's value genuinely changes *while the element is being rendered*. When `is-open` is removed, `opacity` goes from `1` back to `0` — that part's fine and should transition. But there's nothing forcing the browser to run that transition on `display: flex → (implicit)` simultaneously; `display` itself isn't transitionable, so switching away from `display: flex` can, in some browsers, immediately make the element non-renderable before the opacity transition gets to visually play out, especially if no explicit `display` value exists on the base class to keep it "flex" during the fade. Let me fix this properly: give the backdrop an explicit `display: flex` on the base class (not just `.is-open`), and let `hidden`'s `!important`-style Tailwind override handle the true hide, exactly the same pattern that makes `openLightbox` work.

